Looking at this problem, I need to prove:
```
K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y),k) == (if k == j then K.Drop(m,k) else K.Put(K.Drop(m,k),j,y))
```

The functions `Put` and `Drop` are opaque, so I need to use the `A.ObservePut` and `A.ObserveDrop` lemmas to reveal their definitions, and `A.Equal` to prove map equality.

Let me trace through the logic:
- `K.Put(m,k,x)` = `m[k := x]`
- `K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y)` = `m[k := x][j := y]`
- `K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m,k,x),j,y),k)` drops key `k` from `m[k := x][j := y]`

If `k == j`: The result is dropping `k` from `m[k := x][k := y]` = `m[k := y]`, which gives `K.Drop(m,k)`
If `k != j`: The result is dropping `k` from `m[k := x][j := y]`, which gives `K.Put(K.Drop(m,k),j,y)`

I need to reveal the definitions using the observation lemmas and then use `A.Equal` to establish the map equality.

```json
{"HelperA.dfy": "A.ObservePut(m, k, x);
    A.ObservePut(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y);
    A.ObserveDrop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k);
    A.ObserveDrop(m, k);
    if k == j {
      assert K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k) == K.Drop(m, k);
    } else {
      A.ObservePut(K.Drop(m, k), j, y);
      var lhs := K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k);
      var rhs := K.Put(K.Drop(m, k), j, y);
      assert lhs.Keys == rhs.Keys;
      forall q | q in lhs ensures q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q] {
        assert q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q];
      }
      A.Equal(lhs, rhs);
    }", "HelperB.dfy": "A.ObservePut(m, k, x);
    A.ObservePut(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y);
    A.ObserveDrop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k);
    A.ObserveDrop(m, k);
    if k == j {
      assert K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k) == K.Drop(m, k);
    } else {
      A.ObservePut(K.Drop(m, k), j, y);
      var lhs := K.Drop(K.Put(K.Put(m, k, x), j, y), k);
      var rhs := K.Put(K.Drop(m, k), j, y);
      assert lhs.Keys == rhs.Keys;
      forall q | q in lhs ensures q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q] {
        assert q in rhs && lhs[q] == rhs[q];
      }
      A.Equal(lhs, rhs);
    }"}
```

